{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Newly Constructed Multiple Dwellings Law rent-control exemption","requirement":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings are exempt from local rent control ordinances for 30 years after construction is completed; rent-subsidized units may also be exempt from local rent control.","key_value":"30 years","coverage_conditions":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings in New Jersey; the exemption period runs from completion of construction of the building.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":"construction_date","min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Exemption runs 30 years from completion of construction"},"exemptions":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings for 30 years; rents subsidized by governmental funding may also be exempt from local rent control ordinances.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-84.5","quoted_span":"newly constructed multiple dwellings shall be exempt from any local rent control ordinances for a period of 30 years following completion of construction of the building.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Ban on rent increases for condominium or cooperative conversion costs","requirement":"When a building is converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership, the landlord may not raise rents to cover costs resulting solely from the conversion.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All tenants in a building converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership, regardless of protected-tenancy eligibility.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Buildings converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership"},"exemptions":"Increases to cover the cost of new services or amenities are allowed.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31","quoted_span":"If a building is converted to a condominium or cooperative form of ownership, or to fee simple ownership of units, rents may not be increased to cover cos ts resulting solely from the conversion (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act rent increase notice and unconscionability rule","requirement":"A landlord cannot raise the rent mid-lease term; to increase rent the landlord must terminate the old lease and offer a new lease at the increased rent with proper written notice, and the increase must not be unconscionable.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential tenancies in New Jersey; increases must comply with any municipal ordinances governing rent increases.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"A landlord cannot raise the rent mid-lease term. The old lease must be terminated, and the new lease must have the increased rental payment.","interaction":"Rent increases must comply with municipal rent control or rent leveling ordinances.","confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act good cause requirement","requirement":"A residential landlord may recover possession only by tenant consent or through the legal eviction process and must have good cause; a Notice to Quit is required for all good cause evictions except nonpayment of rent.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Dwelling units used as a residence; no residential landlord may evict or fail to renew a written or oral lease without good cause.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":"Nonpayment of rent evictions do not require a Notice to Quit.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"No residential landlord may evict or fail to renew a lease, whether it is a written or an oral lease without good cause.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Security deposit transfer, return and penalty rules","requirement":"On sale or transfer, the new owner must obtain the tenant's deposit and accrued interest and is responsible for investing it, giving notices and returning it; willfully withholding an assisted tenant's deposit is penalized.","key_value":"$500-$2,000 per offense","coverage_conditions":"Residential tenancies in New Jersey; the civil penalty applies where the tenant receives state or federal financial assistance, including welfare or rental assistance.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Special penalty where the tenant receives state or federal financial assistance"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Civil penalty of $500 to $2,000 per offense for willfully withholding an assisted tenant's deposit; unlawful use of deposit monies is a disorderly persons offense with a fine of at least $200, up to 30 days imprisonment, or both.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21","quoted_span":"Any landlord who willfully and intentionally withholds a security deposit made by or on behalf of a tenant who has received financial assistance through any State or federal program, including welfare or rental assistance, may be penalized. The landlord may be liable for a civil penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 for each offense.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"screening_restrictions","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"LAD protection against source-of-lawful-income discrimination","requirement":"The Law Against Discrimination prohibits a landlord or rental agency from refusing to rent or discriminating in housing based on a renter's source of lawful income, and requires equal treatment in renting.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All landlord-tenant relationships in New Jersey, except certain owner-occupied and single-sex housing.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"Excludes owner-occupied two-family dwellings and rooms in owner- or resident-occupied single homes"},"exemptions":"Two-family owner-occupied dwellings, rooms in an owner- or resident-occupied single home, and residences planned exclusively for and occupied by one sex (YMCA, age-restricted housing) as to familial status.","penalty":"Up to $10,000 for a first offense under the LAD.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 10:5-12","quoted_span":"requires equal treatment in the sale or rental of housing regardless of race, creed, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, marital status, civil union status, domestic partnership status, familial status, affectional or sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, mental and physical disability, nationality, or source of lawful income.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"application_screening_fees","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Permitted tenant screening charges and credit report notice","requirement":"A landlord may charge a prospective tenant the cost of a credit or screening report and may request reasonable rental application fees or employment verification; if an application is denied because of the credit report, the applicant must be given the reporting agency's name, address and phone number.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Prospective tenants of New Jersey residential rental units.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"15 U.S.C.A. § 1681m","quoted_span":"The landlord is allowed to charge the tenant for the cost of the report. The landlord may also request reasonable rental application fees or employment verification.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.6,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
